WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
A speech sound screening quickly identifies people who may need further assessment.

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.100
It is not an in-depth evaluation or a diagnosis.

3
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.800
A comprehensive assessment considers relevant history, oral structure and

4
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.700
function, hearing, perception, single words and connected speech.

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.460
Speech sound difficulty may involve perception, planning, articulation or phonology.

6
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.640
Match evidence to the suspected mechanism.

7
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.100
Single-word tests often sample a sound in only one

8
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.920
context and may omit vowels or linguistic varieties.

9
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.980
Connected speech adds error consistency, patterns, rate and intelligibility.

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.600
Analyze the number, type, form and position of errors.

11
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.320
Stimulability asks whether the client can produce an erred

12
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.620
sound correctly with assistance; it provides prognostic information.

13
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.080
It does not automatically erase a disorder.

14
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.060
Developmental sound acquisition, conversational acquisition, consonant-cluster development

15
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.180
and occurrence frequency answer different questions.

16
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.560
Use the relevant table and context.

17
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.460
Do not diagnose from developmental norms alone.

18
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.240
Consider intelligibility, stimulability, perception, error patterns, social

19
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.600
and academic impact, history and language.

20
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:16.160
Phonological processing concerns thinking about, remembering or manipulating sounds;

21
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.440
speech sound production concerns how the words are spoken.

22
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.980
The Week six GFTA activity requires real-time error recording.

23
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.320
Use IPA for substitutions, a dash for an omission, diacritics or

24
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.700
notes for a distortion, and N R for no response.

25
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.560
Follow the appropriate test manual’s prompts and scoring.

26
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.700
No response is not automatically an omitted phoneme.

27
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.200
GFTA findings contribute to a broader profile alongside connected speech,

28
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.833
hearing and oral examination, history and other relevant measures.
